---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

// Claim endpoints come from build-time env so the same bundle can target any
// deployment. The claim routes live on the issuer, next to /v1/onboard.
const issuerBase = import.meta.env.TC_ISSUER_BASE ?? "https://issuer.tracecommons.ai";
const nearNetwork = import.meta.env.TC_NEAR_NETWORK ?? "mainnet";
---

<Base
  title="NEAR Legion invites"
  description="NEAR Legion Vanguard and Ascendant rank holders can claim invite codes to the Trace Commons pilot."
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <meta name="tc-issuer-base" content={issuerBase} />
    <meta name="tc-near-network" content={nearNetwork} />
  </Fragment>

  <h1>Invites for the NEAR Legion</h1>

  <p>
    The pilot is invite-only while the register is being built, and every invite
    so far has been handed out by the operator. This is the first self-serve
    path in: connect a NEAR account holding a
    <a href="https://nearlegion.com">NEAR Legion</a> Vanguard or Ascendant rank,
    prove you control it, and get invite codes to hand out.
  </p>

  <p>
    Rank decides the size of the allotment: <strong>Vanguard gets five</strong>
    redemptions, <strong>Ascendant gets three</strong>. Initiate rank and the
    tradeable NEAR Legion collection cannot claim in this round.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Legion is an adjacent community — people building and arguing about
    decentralized AI — which is roughly the population whose agent traces the
    register most wants. That is the whole reason this door exists.
  </p>

  <noscript>
    <p class="banner">
      Claiming requires JavaScript, because it needs your wallet to sign a
      message. The rest of the site — leaderboard, contributor pages, analytics
      — does not.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div data-legion-claim>
    <p class="muted">Loading…</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What you are actually getting</h2>
  <p>
    One invite code, redeemable a fixed number of times, bound to the NEAR
    account you sign with. One allotment per account. Codes are yours to give
    away — to collaborators, to people whose agent work you would want in the
    register, to whoever you think should be able to see this evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    Contributing is private by default. Someone who redeems your code runs
    Ironclaw locally and sets their own consent scope; nothing about them
    becomes public unless they later choose it from the
    <a href="/profile">profile page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Being straight about the gate</h2>
  <p>
    Rank is a better signal than the tradeable collection, which is why the gate
    moved. The base NEAR Legion collection is described as soulbound but is in
    fact transferable, and minting is open, so holding one proves little. The
    rank credentials are assigned rather than bought, and they do not appear to
    move between accounts — which is what makes them worth gating on.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is still a signal, not a qualification, and we would rather say so than
    imply this is more selective than it is. Ranks are cumulative: everyone
    holding Vanguard also holds the ranks beneath it, so you are always offered
    the largest allotment your rank allows, never the smallest.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Each rank has its own cap.</strong> The counters above are live and
    independent — a full Vanguard round does not close the Ascendant one.
    Treasury and contract accounts are excluded, because the project account
    holds rank credentials of its own and could otherwise claim alongside people.
  </p>

  <h2>What signing does and does not do</h2>
  <p>
    You sign a message, not a transaction. It costs no gas, moves no tokens, and
    grants this site no permission over your account. It is a
    <a href="https://github.com/near/NEPs/blob/master/neps/nep-0413.md">NEP-413</a>
    signature over a one-time challenge we issue, which is how we know the
    account is yours rather than one you typed in.
  </p>
  <p>
    We record a hash of your account ID, and nothing else about you. The raw
    account ID is never stored. That is the same posture as the rest of this
    site — see the <a href="/about/privacy">privacy notes</a>.
  </p>

  <style>
    [data-legion-claim] {
      margin: 1.5rem 0;
      padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 6px;
      background: var(--row);
    }
    [data-legion-claim] .claim-panel > *:first-child {
      margin-top: 0;
    }
    [data-legion-claim] .claim-panel > *:last-child {
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }
    [data-legion-claim] button {
      padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
      font: inherit;
      cursor: pointer;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      background: var(--accent);
      color: var(--bg);
      border-radius: 4px;
    }
    [data-legion-claim] button:hover {
      opacity: 0.9;
    }
    [data-legion-claim] .claim-code code {
      display: block;
      padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
      font-size: 1.25rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      word-break: break-all;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 4px;
      background: var(--bg);
    }
    [data-legion-claim] .warn {
      font-size: 0.95rem;
    }
    [data-legion-claim] .error {
      color: #cf222e;
    }
    @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
      [data-legion-claim] .error {
        color: #ff6a6a;
      }
    }
  </style>

  <script>
    import "../scripts/legion-claim";
  </script>
</Base>
